GLOBAL COGNITIVE INTEROPERABILITY LAYER (GCIL)

A Federated Protocol Architecture for Provenance-Preserving, Sovereignty-Preserving Exchange of Epistemic Bundles Between Heterogeneous AI Agents

Doctoral Thesis — Theoretical Specification v0.2 (Mathematical Foundations)

---

Preface to v0.2

This version is exclusively theoretical. It contains no experiments, no code, no empirical measurements, and no prototype results. It is a mathematical specification with proof obligations, a formal architecture, and a design theory. Every claim is either (i) a definition, (ii) a proposition with proof, (iii) a theorem with proof, (iv) a conjecture with an explicit proof obligation, or (v) an open problem explicitly marked as such.

The v0.2 revision incorporates validated critical advice: the \Pi-NSM-1 interaction is repaired by making policy a journaled object; the corroboration formula is reformulated as a computable lower bound; the scope rule is re-typed; the governance meta-protocol is formalized as a labelled transition system; the stratification rule is re-derived as a dependent type system; the Eigenius attribution is corrected; and the test battery is extended to T0–T12 as theoretical specifications.

---

Part I — Foundations

1. Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Contemporary agent interoperability protocols solve connectivity. They do not solve knowledge. No protocol allows two agents' memories to reconcile a fact phrased two ways, to link related facts held apart, or to reconcile contradictory knowledge without silently discarding one claim. The gap is not transport but epistemic exchange.

This thesis formalizes the Global Cognitive Interoperability Layer (GCIL): a federated protocol architecture for the exchange of epistemic bundles between sovereign agents, preserving the distinction between what is asserted, observed, inferred, contested, and superseded.

1.2 The Central Structure

\boxed{
\mathrm{GCIL} = \langle T, K, P, S, \mathcal{A}, G, \mathcal{H}, \Pi \rangle
}

where T is transport, K knowledge reconciliation, P provenance, S sovereignty, \mathcal{A} the Act alphabet, G the governance transition system, \mathcal{H} the Act Journal, and \Pi the journaled policy state. The governance system acts on the structure:

G : (T, K, P, S, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{H}, \Pi) \rightsquigarrow (T', K', P', S', \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{H}, \Pi')

The arrow \rightsquigarrow is a control relation, formally defined in §13 as a labelled transition system. The v0.1 notation (T,K,P,S) \rtimes G was mathematically imprecise: a semidirect product requires a group acting by automorphisms on another group, and a tuple of protocol components is neither.

1.3 Scope and Non-Claims

\mathrm{GCIL} \neq \text{truth adjudicator}, \quad
\mathrm{GCIL} \neq \text{agent platform}, \quad
\mathrm{GCIL} \neq \text{knowledge fabric}.

The thesis specifies a protocol. It does not claim empirical success. It does not claim that any invariant is preserved under any deployment; it specifies the invariants and the proof obligations that establish their preservation under stated assumptions.

1.4 Epistemic Position

The thesis's central invariant is:

\boxed{
\text{GCIL records epistemic state} \neq \text{GCIL declares truth}
}

Every mathematical object below is defined so that "supported", "replicated", "adjudicated", and "true" are distinct and non-collapsible.

