2. Mathematical Preliminaries

2.1 Notation

· \mathbb{N} denotes \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\}.
· \mathbb{B} denotes \{\bot, \top\}.
· A^* denotes the free monoid on A.
· \mathcal{P}(X) denotes the powerset.
· \Sigma denotes a signature scheme (\mathrm{Gen}, \mathrm{Sign}, \mathrm{Verify}).
· h : \{0,1\}^* \to \{0,1\}^{256} denotes SHA-256.
· \mathrm{Ind} denotes the independence relation of §7.
· \sqsubseteq denotes lattice ordering; \sqcup, \sqcap denote join and meet.
· \to denotes a transition relation; \Rightarrow denotes logical implication.

2.2 Lattices and Orders

Definition 2.1 (Bounded Lattice). A bounded lattice is a tuple (L, \sqsubseteq, \sqcup, \sqcap, \bot, \top) where \sqsubseteq is a partial order on L, every pair x, y \in L has a least upper bound x \sqcup y and a greatest lower bound x \sqcap y, and \bot, \top are the least and greatest elements.

Definition 2.2 (Product Lattice). Given lattices L_1, \ldots, L_n, the product L_1 \times \cdots \times L_n is a lattice under componentwise \sqsubseteq.

Definition 2.3 (Distributive Lattice). L is distributive if for all x, y, z, x \sqcap (y \sqcup z) = (x \sqcap y) \sqcup (x \sqcap z).

2.3 Directed Acyclic Graphs

Definition 2.4 (DAG). A directed acyclic graph is G = (V, E) where E \subseteq V \times V and G contains no directed cycle.

Definition 2.5 (Ancestor Set). For v \in V, \mathrm{Anc}(v) = \{u \in V : u \to^* v, u \neq v\}.

Definition 2.6 (Disjointness). Two vertices u, v have disjoint ancestors if \mathrm{Anc}(u) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(v) = \varnothing.

2.4 Labelled Transition Systems

Definition 2.7 (LTS). A labelled transition system is a tuple \mathcal{L} = (S, \Lambda, \to, s_0) where S is a set of configurations, \Lambda a set of labels, \to \subseteq S \times \Lambda \times S a transition relation, and s_0 \in S the initial configuration.

Definition 2.8 (Invariant). Given \mathcal{L} and I \subseteq S, I is an invariant of \mathcal{L} iff s_0 \in I and for all s \in I, s \xrightarrow{\lambda} s' implies s' \in I.

2.5 Dependent Type Theory (Minimal Fragment)

We use a minimal dependent type theory sufficient to state stratification (§14).

· Types: L_0, L_1, L_2, L_3.
· Universes: \mathcal{U}_0 : \mathcal{U}_1 : \mathcal{U}_2 : \mathcal{U}_3.
· Judgments: \Gamma \vdash x : A (x has type A in context Γ).
· Type-level function: \mathrm{type}(x) = L_n.

