3. The Act Primitive

3.1 Act Tuple

Definition 3.1 (Act). An Act is a tuple

A = (p, \tau, m, \iota, o, \sigma, \kappa, \mathsf{pol})

where p \in \mathcal{P} is the producer, \tau \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} the timestamp, m \in \mathcal{M} the versioned method, \iota \subseteq \mathcal{O} input object references, o \subseteq \mathcal{O} output object references, \sigma \in \{0,1\}^* a signature, \kappa \in \mathcal{K} a PROV-O activity class, and \mathsf{pol} = h(\Pi_{t-1}) the content address of the policy in force.

The Act alphabet is fixed and versioned:

\mathcal{A} = \{\texttt{Genesis}, \texttt{Create}, \texttt{Attach}, \texttt{Challenge}, \texttt{Merge}, \texttt{Split}, \texttt{Link}, \texttt{Retract}, \texttt{UpdateScope}, \texttt{RequestAccess}, \texttt{RefuseAccess}, \texttt{Archive}, \texttt{Reopen}, \texttt{PolicyChange}, \texttt{PolicyEndorse}, \texttt{PolicyEnact}\}

3.2 The Commit Pipeline

Definition 3.2 (Commit Relation). The commit relation is a partial function

\mathrm{Commit} : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \times \Pi \to \mathcal{S} \cup \{\bot\}

such that \mathrm{Commit}(S, A, \Pi) = \bot if any precondition in \mathrm{Pre}(A, \Pi) fails.

Definition 3.3 (Preconditions). For each A \in \mathcal{A}, \mathrm{Pre}(A, \Pi) is a finite set of predicates over S, A, and \Pi. The predicate set is a specification parameter; its extension is a design choice.

3.3 The No-Silent-Mutation Invariant

Invariant NSM-1.

\boxed{
\mathrm{Commit}(S_t, A, \Pi_t) = S_{t+1} \neq \bot \;\Rightarrow\; A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1}
}

Invariant NSM-2 (Contrapositive Enforcement).

\boxed{
\Delta S \neq \varnothing \;\land\; \neg\exists A \in \mathcal{H} : A.\mathrm{effect} = \Delta S \;\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{Commit} = \bot
}

Proposition 3.1. NSM-1 and NSM-2 are equivalent.

Proof. Immediate by contraposition. If NSM-1 holds and no journaled Act produces \Delta S, then the antecedent of NSM-2 is satisfied, so Commit returns \bot; if Commit returns non-\bot, then some Act was journaled. ∎

3.4 The Act Journal

Definition 3.4 (Journal). A journal is a finite sequence

\mathcal{H}_t = (A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_t) \in \mathcal{A}^*

with the append-only property:

\mathcal{H}_t \sqsubseteq \mathcal{H}_{t+1}

where \sqsubseteq denotes sequence prefix.

Definition 3.5 (State as Fold). The state function is

S_t = \mathrm{fold}(\mathcal{H}_t, S_0, \Pi_0)

where \mathrm{fold} applies \mathrm{Commit} sequentially.

Proposition 3.2 (Determinism). If \Pi_t is determined by \mathcal{H}_t (see §4), then S_t is uniquely determined by (\mathcal{H}_t, S_0, \Pi_0).

Proof. By induction on |\mathcal{H}_t|. Base: S_0 is given. Step: assume S_i and \Pi_i are determined by \mathcal{H}_i. Then A_{i+1} \in \mathcal{H}_{i+1} and \Pi_i determine S_{i+1} = \mathrm{Commit}(S_i, A_{i+1}, \Pi_i) uniquely, since \mathrm{Commit} is a partial function. ∎

