4. Policy as Journaled State

4.1 The Π-Break Problem (Validated Critical Advice)

The v0.1.1 formulation S_t = \mathrm{Replay}(\mathcal{H}_t, \Pi) made state depend on a policy set \Pi outside the journal. This violates NSM-1: a change to \Pi alters state without an Act. The v0.2 repair makes \Pi a journaled object.

4.2 Policy Structure

Definition 4.1 (Policy).

\Pi = (\pi_{vis}, \pi_{adm}, \pi_{gov}, v, h_{\mathrm{parent}})

where:

· \pi_{vis} is the visibility policy (who sees what),
· \pi_{adm} is the admission policy (Act preconditions),
· \pi_{gov} = (k, \tau_{\mathrm{win}}, p_{\max}) is the governance parameter tuple,
· v \in \mathbb{N} is the version,
· h_{\mathrm{parent}} \in \{0,1\}^{256} is the content address of the parent policy.

4.3 Policy Acts

Definition 4.2 (PolicyChange). A PolicyChange Act proposes a diff \delta against a target component of \Pi with base h(\Pi_{t-1}), producing a pending proposal \rho.

Definition 4.3 (PolicyEndorse). A PolicyEndorse Act attaches a producer's endorsement to \rho.

Definition 4.4 (PolicyEnact). A PolicyEnact Act outputs \Pi' = \mathrm{Apply}(\Pi, \delta) with v' = v + 1 and h_{\mathrm{parent}} = h(\Pi).

4.4 Enactment Conditions

E1 (Quorum). At least k_{\mathrm{target}} pairwise-independent endorsers (§7), each with influence I_i < p_{\max}. For \pi_{gov} amendments, k_{\mathrm{gov}} > k and \tau'_{\mathrm{win}} > \tau_{\mathrm{win}}.

E2 (Contestation). \tau_{\mathrm{enact}} \geq \tau_{\mathrm{prop}} + \tau_{\mathrm{win}}, and every Challenge against \rho is adjudicated.

E3 (Fresh base). \rho.\mathrm{base} = h(\Pi_{t-1}).

E4 (Core immunity). \delta does not touch

I_{\mathrm{core}} = \{\text{NSM-1}, \text{append-only}, \text{Rule St}, \text{Refuse}, \text{P2}\}

4.5 Policy Invariants

Invariant P1 (Policy is state).

\Delta \Pi \neq \varnothing \;\Rightarrow\; \exists\, \texttt{PolicyEnact} \in \mathcal{H}

Invariant P2 (Non-retroactivity). A PolicyEnact at index j affects validity only for indices > j. For all i \leq j, \mathrm{Valid}(A_i) is judged under \Pi_{i-1} forever.

Invariant P3 (No self-ratification). \mathrm{Valid}(\texttt{PolicyEnact}_j) is evaluated only under \Pi_{j-1}, never under the \Pi' it creates.

Proposition 4.1 (Policy determinism).

\Pi_t = \mathrm{fold}(\{\texttt{PolicyEnact} \in \mathcal{H}_{\leq t}\}, \Pi_0)

where \Pi_0 is established by a Genesis Act A_0.

Proof. By induction on t. Base: \Pi_0 is the initial policy. Step: if A_{t+1} is not a PolicyEnact, \Pi_{t+1} = \Pi_t. If A_{t+1} is a PolicyEnact, \Pi_{t+1} = \mathrm{Apply}(\Pi_t, \delta) is uniquely determined by A_{t+1} and \Pi_t. ∎

Corollary 4.1. S_t = (D_t, \Pi_t) is computable from \mathcal{H}_{\leq t} alone. NSM-1 holds with S = (D, \Pi).

4.6 Policy Forks

Definition 4.5 (Fork). Two PolicyEnact Acts with the same base and target but different \delta create a policy fork, recorded as \mathrm{divergent}(\Pi) with no automatic resolution.

Federation rule. Acts whose \mathsf{pol} belongs to the other branch are refused (G5, reason POLICY_BRANCH_MISMATCH). Reunification is a PolicyChange whose base set contains both heads, endorsed by both instances.

Proposition 4.2. Policy forks are detectable and non-silent.

Proof. By construction, the base hash of each PolicyEnact is recorded. Two Acts with the same base but different \delta produce different output hashes; their joint presence in the journal is a witness of divergence. Detection is a decision procedure over \mathcal{H}. ∎

