Part II — The Epistemic Bundle

5. Bundle Structure

Definition 5.1 (Epistemic Bundle).

EB = (C, E, D, S, P, U, A, R)

where C is the claim, E the evidence set, D the derivation, S the scope, P the provenance, U the uncertainty tuple, A the alternatives, R the revision history.

5.1 Claim

Definition 5.2 (Claim). C = (\mathrm{id}, \phi, \mathrm{key}) where \mathrm{id} is a content address, \phi a proposition in a chosen formal language, and \mathrm{key} a scoped claim-key for admission.

5.2 Evidence

Definition 5.3 (Evidence). E = \{e_1, \ldots, e_n\} with e_i = (\mathrm{ref}, \mathrm{polarity}, \mathrm{weight}) and \mathrm{polarity} \in \{+, -\}.

5.3 Derivation

Definition 5.4 (Derivation). D = (m, \iota) where m is the method (from \mathcal{M}) and \iota the input references.

5.4 Provenance

Definition 5.5 (Provenance). P is a sub-DAG of the global Act DAG, rooted at the Act that produced the bundle.

5.5 Revision History

Definition 5.6 (Revision). R = \{(A_i, \tau_i, \mathrm{status}_i)\} ordered by \tau.

Proposition 5.1. \mathrm{status}(C, t, \gamma) is a fold over R.

Proof. By construction of §3.5. ∎

