6. Uncertainty Calculus

6.1 Decomposition

Definition 6.1 (Decomposed Uncertainty).

U = (U_C, U_E, U_D, U_S)

where:

· U_C \in \mathcal{I}_C is the uncertainty on the proposition,
· U_E \in \mathcal{I}_E is the quality of the evidence,
· U_D \in \mathcal{I}_D is the uncertainty of the derivation,
· U_S \in \mathcal{I}_S is the uncertainty on the scope.

Each \mathcal{I}_X is a lattice of intervals or distributions, not a scalar.

Proposition 6.1 (Non-collapse). There is no total order on \mathcal{I}_C \times \mathcal{I}_E \times \mathcal{I}_D \times \mathcal{I}_S that preserves the epistemic distinctions of GCIL.

Proof. The four intervals are independent dimensions. A total order would impose a comparison between, e.g., high propositional confidence with poor evidence and low propositional confidence with strong evidence. Any such comparison collapses a distinction GCIL is required to preserve by the invariant of §1.4. ∎

6.2 Interval Semantics

Definition 6.2 (Interval). \mathcal{I} = \{[a, b] : 0 \leq a \leq b \leq 1\} \cup \{[a, b) : 0 \leq a \leq b \leq 1\}.

Definition 6.3 (Interval Order). [a, b] \sqsubseteq [c, d] iff c \leq a and b \leq d.

Proposition 6.2. (\mathcal{I}, \sqsubseteq) is a lattice.

Proof. Join is [\min(a,c), \max(b,d)], meet is [\max(a,c), \min(b,d)]. Both are well-defined since a \leq b and c \leq d. ∎

