7. Independence-Conditioned Corroboration

7.1 The Independence Problem

The v0.1.1 formula

\mathrm{corrob}(C) = |\{E_i : \forall j \neq i, \mathrm{Ind}(E_i, E_j)\}|

was too strict: requiring every E_i to be independent of all others means two dependent sources both drop out, and \mathrm{corrob} = 0.

7.2 Provenance DAG

Definition 7.1 (Evidence Ancestry). For each e_i \in E, let \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) be the set of Acts in the derivation DAG that produced e_i.

Definition 7.2 (Ancestor Independence).

\mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j) \iff \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) = \varnothing

7.3 Reformulated Corroboration

Definition 7.3 (Corroboration).

\boxed{
\mathrm{corrob}(C) = \max\{|S| : S \subseteq E, \forall e_i, e_j \in S, i \neq j, \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j)\}
}

7.4 Computability

Proposition 7.1. Computing \mathrm{corrob}(C) exactly is NP-hard.

Proof. The problem is the maximum independent set problem on the dependency graph G_E = (E, \{(e_i, e_j) : \neg \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j)\}). Maximum independent set is NP-hard on general graphs. Reduction: given an arbitrary graph G, construct evidence with \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) = \{i\} \cup \mathrm{Neigh}(i). Then \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j) iff e_i, e_j are non-adjacent in G. Thus \mathrm{corrob} = \alpha(G), the independence number. ∎

Definition 7.4 (Computable Lower Bound). A polynomial-time lower bound is

\boxed{
\mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) = \left| \bigsqcup_{e_i \in E} \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \right| / \max_{e_i} |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|
}

where \bigsqcup denotes the disjoint union.

Proposition 7.2. \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) \leq \mathrm{corrob}(C).

Proof. Each element of \bigsqcup \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) belongs to exactly one \mathrm{Anc}(e_i). If two evidence items share an ancestor, the disjoint union overcounts; the maximum independent set is at least the number of evidence items with pairwise-disjoint ancestors, which is bounded below by the ratio given. ∎

Proposition 7.3. When all evidence items have pairwise-disjoint ancestry, \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) = |E|.

Proof. Under disjointness, \bigsqcup \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) has size \sum |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|, and the maximum |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| is at least 1. ∎

7.5 Citation-Chain Laundering

Definition 7.5 (Laundering). A citation chain e_i \to e_j \to e_k is a laundering if e_i claims independent support but its ancestry overlaps e_j.

Proposition 7.4. Laundering is detectable in polynomial time in |\mathcal{H}|.

Proof. Build the ancestry DAG. Intersections of ancestor sets are computable in O(|V| + |E|) per pair via BFS/DFS. The decision "is this evidence item laundering?" is a membership test. ∎

