8. Scope Algebra

8.1 Scope as Tuple

Definition 8.1 (Scope).

S(C) = (S_{\mathrm{time}}, S_{\mathrm{space}}, S_{\mathrm{pop}}, S_{\mathrm{cond}})

where each component is an interval, a set, or a predicate.

8.2 Scope Order

Definition 8.2 (Scope Refinement). S \sqsubseteq S' iff each component of S is contained in the corresponding component of S'. We write S \sqsubseteq S' as "S is narrower than S'".

Proposition 8.1. (\mathcal{S}, \sqsubseteq) is a lattice.

Proof. Componentwise containment of intervals and sets is a lattice. Product of lattices is a lattice. ∎

8.3 The Re-Typed Scope Rule (Validated Critical Advice)

The v0.1.1 rule "Scope_new ⊆ Scope_old ⇒ UpdateScope ∈ 𝒜" was trivially true since \mathcal{A} is fixed.

Definition 8.3 (UpdateScope Acts). Every change to S(C) must be produced by an UpdateScope Act with \mathrm{effect}: S \mapsto S'.

Definition 8.4 (Narrowing vs. Widening).

· Narrowing: S' \sqsubseteq S. Requires only an UpdateScope Act.
· Widening: S' \not\sqsubseteq S. Requires an UpdateScope Act and a Challenge Act or a new Attach Act.

Proposition 8.2 (Well-typed change). For all scope changes, there is an UpdateScope Act in the journal.

Proof. Direct from Definition 8.3. ∎

Proposition 8.3 (Widening justification). A widening without Challenge or Attach violates preconditions.

Proof. By construction of Pre(UpdateScope, Π): the precondition set includes S' \sqsubseteq S \lor (\exists \texttt{Challenge} \lor \exists \texttt{Attach}). If the latter disjunct fails, the precondition fails and Commit returns \bot. ∎

