Part IV — Governance and Architecture

13. Governance as Labelled Transition System

13.1 The v0.1 Informality (Validated Critical Advice)

"Control loop" was informal. v0.2 formalizes G as an LTS.

13.2 Definition

Definition 13.1 (Governance LTS).

\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{S}_G, \Lambda_G, \to_G, s_0^G, I_G)

where:

· \mathcal{S}_G = \mathcal{S} \times \Pi is the configuration space (state + policy),
· \Lambda_G = \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{pol}} \cup \{\mathrm{NoOp}\} is the label set,
· \to_G \subseteq \mathcal{S}_G \times \Lambda_G \times \mathcal{S}_G is the transition relation,
· s_0^G = (S_0, \Pi_0) is the initial configuration,
· I_G = \{\text{NSM-1}, \text{P1}, \text{P2}, \text{P3}, \text{Rule St}, \text{Refuse}\} is the invariant set.

13.3 Transitions

For A \in \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{pol}}:

(S, \Pi) \xrightarrow{A} (S', \Pi') \iff A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1} \land S' = \mathrm{Commit}(S, A, \Pi) \land \Pi' = \mathrm{PolicyUpdate}(\Pi, A)

For NoOp:

(S, \Pi) \xrightarrow{\mathrm{NoOp}} (S, \Pi)

13.4 Invariant Preservation

Theorem 13.1. \mathcal{G} preserves I_G.

Proof. Let I \in I_G. We show I is an invariant of \mathcal{G}.

· NSM-1: by Definition 3.2 and Definition 13.1, every transition produces an Act in the journal. ∎
· P1: by Definition 4.4, \Pi' \neq \Pi only if A = \texttt{PolicyEnact}. ∎
· P2: by Definition 4.5 (non-retroactivity), enactments affect only future indices. The transition relation does not modify past Acts. ∎
· P3: by Definition 4.5 (no self-ratification), enactment validity is judged under the prior policy. The transition relation does not evaluate validity under the new policy. ∎
· Rule St: enforced by the type checker of §14; the LTS does not permit type-invalid Acts. ∎
· Refuse: for every Act A, the transition relation permits a dual Refuse(A) Act recorded in the journal. ∎

Corollary 13.1. Every reachable configuration of \mathcal{G} satisfies I_G.

Proof. Direct from Theorem 13.1 by induction on transition length. ∎

13.5 Governance Rules as Transition Constraints

G1 (Policy Change / Quorum). A transition labeled PolicyEnact requires the precondition set E1–E4 of §4.4.

G2 (Contestation).

\mathrm{Freeze}(A_i) \Rightarrow A_i \not\to \mathrm{Modify}(A_i)

but

\mathrm{NewEvidence}(C) \Rightarrow \mathrm{Reopen}(C)

G3 (Anti-capture). For every domain d, \forall i \in \mathcal{P}: I_i(d) < p_{\max}(d).

G4 (Policy Replay).

\mathrm{Replay}_k(\mathcal{H}, \Pi') \to (\mathrm{State}', \mathrm{Rej})

for 0 \leq k \leq |\mathcal{H}|:

· Indices < k use the recorded \Pi_i;
· from k on, \Pi' replaces the recorded trajectory;
· Acts invalid under \Pi' go into \mathrm{Rej}, reported, not silently dropped;
· Replay never writes to \mathcal{H}.

G5 (Refusal). \mathrm{Refuse}(A_i) \in \mathcal{H} with producer, reason, timestamp.

13.6 The Residual Gap

The validator's code is part of the trusted computing base. Attestation (e.g., TRACE) can bind it to a hardware root of trust, but does not eliminate the trust assumption. The v0.1 claim that stratification "resolves the self-hosting paradox" is withdrawn. The tower stops at Level 3; Level 4 would require an assertion about Level 3, which P3 forbids.

