\Phi : \mathcal{EB} \times \mathcal{EB} \to [0, 1]

where \Phi(EB_0, EB_n) measures the fraction of epistemic distinctions preserved.

Definition 17.2 (Distinction Set).

\Delta(EB) = \{(\mathrm{dim}, v) : \mathrm{dim} \in \mathcal{D}, v \in \mathrm{Val}(\mathrm{dim})\}

where \mathcal{D} = \{\mathrm{prov}, \mathrm{repro}, \mathrm{corrob}, \mathrm{scope}, \mathrm{dispute}\}.

Definition 17.3 (Conservation).

\Phi(EB_0, EB_n) = \frac{|\Delta(EB_0) \cap \Delta(EB_n)|}{|\Delta(EB_0)|}

17.2 Main Theorem

Theorem 17.1 (Conservation Bound). For a transformation sequence with no silent mutation, no semantic collapse, and no false merge,

\Phi(EB_0, EB_n) = 1

Proof. Under the three conditions, every distinction in EB_0 is preserved in EB_n: no silent mutation means every change is journaled; no semantic collapse means the type system of §14 forbids distinction erasure; no false merge means the reconciliation of §10 preserves all admitted distinctions. Thus \Delta(EB_0) \subseteq \Delta(EB_n). ∎

Corollary 17.1. Any violation of the three conditions produces \Phi(EB_0, EB_n) < 1, with the exact deficit bounded by the number of erased distinctions.

17.3 The Research Question, Formalized

\boxed{
P(\Phi(EB_0, EB_n) = 1 \mid n, \mathrm{partition}, \mathcal{X}, \mathrm{heterogeneity}, \Pi)
}

This is the probability that a transformation sequence of length n under partition, adversary \mathcal{X}, heterogeneity, and policy \Pi preserves all epistemic distinctions. It is a theoretical quantity in v0.2; it becomes empirical in v0.3.

