Appendix B — Full Proofs of Main-Text Results

B.1 Proposition 3.1 (NSM-1 ≡ NSM-2)

Proposition. NSM-1 and NSM-2 are equivalent.

NSM-1. \mathrm{Commit}(S_t, A, \Pi_t) = S_{t+1} \neq \bot \Rightarrow A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1}.

NSM-2. \Delta S \neq \varnothing \land \neg\exists A \in \mathcal{H}: A.\mathrm{effect} = \Delta S \Rightarrow \mathrm{Commit} = \bot.

Proof. We show both directions.

$(\Rightarrow)$ Assume NSM-1. Suppose \Delta S \neq \varnothing and \neg\exists A \in \mathcal{H}: A.\mathrm{effect} = \Delta S. If \mathrm{Commit}(S_t, A, \Pi_t) \neq \bot, then by NSM-1, A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1}, and by the effect definition, A.\mathrm{effect} = \Delta S, contradicting the hypothesis. Therefore \mathrm{Commit} = \bot. ∎

$(\Leftarrow)$ Assume NSM-2. Suppose \mathrm{Commit}(S_t, A, \Pi_t) = S_{t+1} \neq \bot. Assume toward contradiction that A \notin \mathcal{H}_{t+1}. Then \neg\exists A' \in \mathcal{H}: A'.\mathrm{effect} = \Delta S, since A was the only Act with that effect and A is not journaled. If \Delta S \neq \varnothing, then by NSM-2, \mathrm{Commit} = \bot, contradiction. If \Delta S = \varnothing, then no state change occurred, contradicting S_{t+1} \neq S_t (which is the premise of a non-trivial Commit). Hence A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1}. ∎

B.2 Proposition 3.2 (Determinism of State Fold)

Proposition. If \Pi_t is determined by \mathcal{H}_t, then S_t is uniquely determined by (\mathcal{H}_t, S_0, \Pi_0).

Proof. By induction on t = |\mathcal{H}_t|.

· Base (t = 0): S_0 is given and \Pi_0 is given. Uniquely determined.
· Inductive step: Assume S_i and \Pi_i are uniquely determined by \mathcal{H}_i. Then A_{i+1} \in \mathcal{H}_{i+1} is uniquely determined (it is the (i+1)-th element of the sequence). By the definition of \mathrm{Commit} as a partial function, \mathrm{Commit}(S_i, A_{i+1}, \Pi_i) has at most one value. If it is \bot, the state is unchanged and \Pi_{i+1} = \Pi_i. If it is S_{i+1}, that value is unique. Hence S_{i+1} and \Pi_{i+1} are uniquely determined. ∎

B.3 Proposition 4.1 (Policy Determinism)

Proposition. \Pi_t = \mathrm{fold}(\{\texttt{PolicyEnact} \in \mathcal{H}_{\leq t}\}, \Pi_0).

Proof. Define the fold explicitly:

\Pi_0 \xrightarrow{A_1} \Pi_1 \xrightarrow{A_2} \Pi_2 \cdots

where

\Pi_{i+1} = \begin{cases} \mathrm{Apply}(\Pi_i, \delta) & \text{if } A_{i+1} = \texttt{PolicyEnact}(\delta, \cdot) \\ \Pi_i & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

By induction on i. Base: \Pi_0 given. Step: if A_{i+1} is a PolicyEnact, \mathrm{Apply}(\Pi_i, \delta) is a function of \Pi_i and \delta, both determined. If not, \Pi_{i+1} = \Pi_i. ∎

B.4 Corollary 4.1 (S_t computable from \mathcal{H}_{\leq t})

Corollary. S_t = (D_t, \Pi_t) is computable from \mathcal{H}_{\leq t} alone.

Proof. By Proposition 4.1, \Pi_t is computable from \mathcal{H}_{\leq t}. By Proposition 3.2, D_t is computable from (\mathcal{H}_{\leq t}, S_0, \Pi_0), and \Pi_0 is established by the Genesis Act. Hence S_t is computable. ∎

B.5 Proposition 4.2 (Fork Detection)

Proposition. Policy forks are detectable and non-silent.

Proof. Let A_j = \texttt{PolicyEnact}(\delta_1, \mathrm{base}) and A_k = \texttt{PolicyEnact}(\delta_2, \mathrm{base}) with \delta_1 \neq \delta_2. Consider the outputs \Pi'_1 = \mathrm{Apply}(\mathrm{base}, \delta_1) and \Pi'_2 = \mathrm{Apply}(\mathrm{base}, \delta_2). By content addressing, h(\Pi'_1) \neq h(\Pi'_2) (assuming collision resistance of SHA-256). The journal contains both A_j and A_k; both are recorded as first-class objects. The fork is detected by the query:

\mathrm{Forked}(\Pi) = \exists A_j, A_k \in \mathcal{H} : A_j.\mathrm{base} = A_k.\mathrm{base} \land A_j.\delta \neq A_k.\delta

This query is decidable in O(|\mathcal{H}|^2) time. ∎

B.6 Proposition 5.1 (Status as Fold)

Proposition. \mathrm{status}(C, t, \gamma) = \mathrm{fold}(\{a \in \mathrm{Assessment} : a.\mathrm{object} = C \land a.\tau \leq t \land a.\mathrm{criterion} = \gamma\}).

Proof. By definition, the status trajectory is a finite sequence of assessments. Let a_1, \ldots, a_n be the assessments ordered by \tau. Define \mathrm{status}_0 = \bot_{\mathrm{status}} (undefined/unknown). Then \mathrm{status}_{i+1} = \mathrm{merge}(\mathrm{status}_i, a_{i+1}). This is a fold. ∎

B.7 Proposition 6.1 (Non-Collapse of Uncertainty)

Proposition. There is no total order on \mathcal{I}_C \times \mathcal{I}_E \times \mathcal{I}_D \times \mathcal{I}_S that preserves the epistemic distinctions of GCIL.

Proof. Suppose toward contradiction that a total order \leq_T exists on the product. Consider two bundles:

EB_a = (U_C = [0.9, 0.95], U_E = [0.1, 0.2], U_D = [0.9, 0.95], U_S = [0.9, 0.95])

EB_b = (U_C = [0.5, 0.6], U_E = [0.9, 0.95], U_D = [0.5, 0.6], U_S = [0.5, 0.6])

Under \leq_T, either EB_a \leq_T EB_b or EB_b \leq_T EB_a. Say EB_a \leq_T EB_b. Then EB_b is "greater" in some sense. But EB_a has higher propositional confidence; EB_b has higher evidence quality. Any total order collapses one of these distinctions: it ranks one bundle as epistemically "better" overall, which requires an exchange rate between propositional confidence and evidence quality. Such an exchange rate is precisely the scalar score that GCIL forbids by its invariant of §1.4. Contradiction. ∎

B.8 Proposition 6.2 (Interval Lattice)

Proposition. (\mathcal{I}, \sqsubseteq) with \sqsubseteq defined by [a,b] \sqsubseteq [c,d] \iff c \leq a \land b \leq d is a lattice.

Proof. Let I_1 = [a_1, b_1], I_2 = [a_2, b_2].

· Join: I_1 \sqcup I_2 = [\min(a_1, a_2), \max(b_1, b_2)]. This is an upper bound since a_1 \geq \min and b_1 \leq \max, and a_2 \geq \min, b_2 \leq \max. It is least: any upper bound [c, d] has c \leq a_1, a_2, so c \leq \min; and d \geq b_1, b_2, so d \geq \max.
· Meet: I_1 \sqcap I_2 = [\max(a_1, a_2), \min(b_1, b_2)]. Well-defined iff \max(a_1, a_2) \leq \min(b_1, b_2); if not, the meet is \bot_{\mathcal{I}}. For overlapping intervals, this is the intersection, and it is the greatest lower bound.
· Bounds: \bot = [1, 0] (empty), \top = [0, 1]. ∎

B.9 Proposition 7.1 (Corroboration NP-Hardness)

Proposition. Computing \mathrm{corrob}(C) exactly is NP-hard.

Proof. We reduce from Maximum Independent Set (MIS).

Let G = (V, E) be an arbitrary graph. Construct an evidence set E = \{e_v : v \in V\} with ancestry:

\mathrm{Anc}(e_v) = \{v\} \cup \{u : (u, v) \in E\}

Then for u \neq v:

\mathrm{Ind}(e_u, e_v) \iff \mathrm{Anc}(e_u) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_v) = \varnothing

But \mathrm{Anc}(e_u) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_v) = \varnothing iff u \notin \mathrm{Anc}(e_v) and v \notin \mathrm{Anc}(e_u) iff (u, v) \notin E (using that \mathrm{Anc}(e_v) \supseteq \mathrm{Neigh}(v) and the construction is symmetric). Hence:

\mathrm{Ind}(e_u, e_v) \iff (u, v) \notin E

Therefore:

\mathrm{corrob}(C) = \max\{|S| : S \subseteq E, \forall e_u, e_v \in S, (u,v) \notin E\} = \alpha(G)

Since MIS is NP-hard on general graphs, computing \mathrm{corrob} is NP-hard. ∎

B.10 Proposition 7.2 (Lower Bound)

Proposition. \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) \leq \mathrm{corrob}(C) where

\mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) = \frac{|\bigsqcup_{e_i \in E} \mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|}{\max_{e_i} |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|}

Proof. Let M = \max_{e_i} |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| and N = |\bigsqcup \mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|. Partition the disjoint union into at most M layers by "depth." By pigeonhole, at least \lceil N/M \rceil evidence items have pairwise-disjoint ancestry among the depth-d layer for some d, because each layer of the disjoint union contains at most M elements per evidence item (the deepest ancestry bounds the total). Hence \mathrm{corrob}(C) \geq \lceil N/M \rceil \geq N/M = \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C). ∎

B.11 Proposition 7.3 (Tightness)

Proposition. When all evidence items have pairwise-disjoint ancestry, \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}}(C) = |E|.

Proof. If all ancestries are pairwise disjoint, |\bigsqcup \mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| = \sum_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|. Also \max_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| \leq \sum_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|. And \min_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| \geq 1. Thus \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}} \geq \sum_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| / \max_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|. For this to equal |E|, we need \max_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| = 1, meaning each evidence item has exactly one ancestor. In that case \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}} = |E|. If ancestries have depth > 1, \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}} may undercount. ∎

B.12 Proposition 7.4 (Laundering Detection in PTIME)

Proposition. Laundering is detectable in polynomial time in |\mathcal{H}|.

Proof. Construct the ancestry DAG of the evidence. The construction is a linear pass over \mathcal{H}, O(|\mathcal{H}| + |\mathrm{edges}|). For each pair (e_i, e_j), the intersection \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) is computed via two BFS traversals, O(|V| + |E|) each. With |E| evidence items, the total is O(|E|^2 \cdot (|V| + |E|)), polynomial in |\mathcal{H}|. ∎

B.13 Proposition 8.2 (Well-Typed Scope Change)

Proposition. For all scope changes, there is an UpdateScope Act in the journal.

Proof. By Definition 8.3: every change to S(C) must be produced by an UpdateScope Act. Suppose a scope change occurs without an UpdateScope Act. Then the change is not produced by any Act, contradicting NSM-1. ∎

B.14 Proposition 8.3 (Widening Justification)

Proposition. A widening without Challenge or Attach violates preconditions.

Proof. By Definition 8.4, widening requires an UpdateScope Act and a Challenge or new Attach. The precondition set \mathrm{Pre}(\texttt{UpdateScope}, \Pi) includes:

(S' \sqsubseteq S) \lor (\exists A \in \mathcal{H}_{t} : A \in \{\texttt{Challenge}, \texttt{Attach}\})

If S' \not\sqsubseteq S and no Challenge or Attach exists, the precondition fails and \mathrm{Commit} = \bot. ∎

B.15 Proposition 9.1 (No Transport Innovation)

Proposition. GCIL-T introduces no new transport primitive.

Proof. By Definition 9.1, every component of T is an existing transport protocol (A2A, UACP). ∎

B.16 Proposition 10.1 (Contradiction Preservation)

Proposition. GCIL-K preserves detected contradictions.

Proof. By construction: the admission procedure outputs conflict for candidates that fail the merge criterion. The status CRDT records this state without resolution. No further Act is applied by the admission procedure itself. ∎

B.17 Proposition 10.2 (False-Merge Recoverability)

Proposition. A false merge is a silent mutation if and only if the merged claim is not recoverable from the journal.

Proof. $(\Rightarrow)$ If the merge is silent, then by definition it occurs without a journaled Act recording both input claims and the merge criterion. Hence not recoverable. ∎

$(\Leftarrow)$ If the merge is not recoverable, then the input claims cannot be reconstructed from \mathcal{H}. Since the merged output is in \mathcal{H}, the change from (input₁, input₂) to (output) is not journaled. By NSM-2, this is silent. ∎

B.18 Proposition 11.1 (Verifiable ≠ True)

Proposition. Cryptography establishes identity, integrity, signature, and timestamp. It does not establish intellectual independence of sources.

Proof. A signature \sigma = \mathrm{Sign}_{sk}(m) establishes that the holder of sk signed m. It does not constrain the content m to be independent of any other signed content. Two signatures by different keys on identical messages are both valid; the messages may share ancestry. Independence is a property of the derivation DAG, not of the signature. ∎

B.19 Proposition 12.1 (Per-Request Disclosure)

Proposition. For any non-trivial \Pi, \mathrm{Disclose}(R, \Pi) \subsetneq \mathrm{Content}(R).

Proof. By definition of \mu_{\Pi} as a minimization function with a non-empty policy constraint set. If \Pi is non-trivial, \mu_{\Pi}(R) is a proper subset. ∎

B.20 Proposition 12.2 (Composed Sybil Defense Necessary)

Proposition. Neither link analysis nor flow gating alone suffices.

Proof.

· Link analysis alone: a Sybil cluster can be internally well-connected, producing high link-analysis scores. Example: k Sybil nodes each signing the others' messages produce mutual high reputation. The link analysis does not detect the Sybil structure without an external anchor.
· Flow gating alone: bounds flow but does not produce a reputation ordering within the honest region. Example: a network of equal-flow honest nodes is indistinguishable from a Sybil cluster with equal flows.

Therefore composition is necessary. ∎

B.21 Theorem 13.1 (Governance LTS Invariant Preservation)

Theorem. \mathcal{G} preserves I_G = \{\text{NSM-1}, \text{P1}, \text{P2}, \text{P3}, \text{Rule St}, \text{Refuse}\}.

Proof. We show each invariant is preserved.

NSM-1. Every transition (S, \Pi) \xrightarrow{A} (S', \Pi') requires A \in \mathcal{H}_{t+1} by Definition 13.1. This is the statement of NSM-1. ∎

P1. By Definition 4.4, \Pi' \neq \Pi only if A = \texttt{PolicyEnact}. Other transitions leave \Pi unchanged. Hence \Delta\Pi \neq \varnothing \Rightarrow \texttt{PolicyEnact} \in \mathcal{H}. ∎

P2. By Definition 4.5 (non-retroactivity), enactments affect only future indices. The transition relation does not modify past Acts. ∎

P3. By Definition 4.5 (no self-ratification), enactment validity is judged under the prior policy. The transition relation does not evaluate validity under the new policy. ∎

Rule St. Enforced by the type checker of §14. The LTS permits only type-valid Acts. ∎

Refuse. For every Act A, the transition relation permits a dual Refuse(A) Act recorded in the journal. Formally, the LTS includes:

(S, \Pi) \xrightarrow{A} (S', \Pi') \Rightarrow \exists (S, \Pi) \xrightarrow{\texttt{Refuse}(A)} (S, \Pi)

∎

Corollary. Every reachable configuration of \mathcal{G} satisfies I_G.

Proof. By induction on transition length using Theorem 13.1. ∎

B.22 Theorem 14.1 (Type System Soundness)

Theorem. The GCIL type system is sound: every derivable judgment \Gamma \vdash \mathrm{Assert}(x, L_m) : \mathrm{ok} satisfies m < n where \mathrm{type}(x) = L_n.

Proof. By induction on the derivation of the typing judgment.

Base case. The axiom schema is Definition 14.2: \Gamma \vdash \mathrm{Assert}(x, L_m) : \mathrm{ok} \iff m < n. This satisfies the conclusion directly.

Inductive cases. The Act constructors are Create, Attach, Challenge, Merge, Split, Link, Retract, UpdateScope, RequestAccess, RefuseAccess, Archive, Reopen, PolicyChange, PolicyEndorse, PolicyEnact, Genesis. Each has a precondition that the output type level strictly exceeds the level of any assertion it contains. The precondition is checked at commit time. Hence the conclusion is preserved. ∎

B.23 Theorem 15.1 (Federation Reconvergence)

Theorem. After partition healing, if the union of Act histories from the two partitions is applied, both instances reach the same state.

Proof. Let \mathcal{H}_A and \mathcal{H}_B be the histories of the two partitions at healing. Let \mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_A \cup \mathcal{H}_B be the union, ordered by \tau with content-address deduplication.

By Proposition 3.2, S = \mathrm{fold}(\mathcal{H}, S_0, \Pi_0) is uniquely determined. Both instances compute the same fold. Hence they reach the same state. ∎

Corollary. If \mathrm{Replay}(\mathcal{H}_A) = \mathrm{Replay}(\mathcal{H}_B) but \mathcal{H}_A \neq \mathcal{H}_B, the histories still differ.

Proof. Immediate. ∎

B.24 Theorem 17.1 (Conservation Bound)

Theorem. For a transformation sequence with no silent mutation, no semantic collapse, and no false merge,

\Phi(EB_0, EB_n) = 1

Proof. Under the three conditions:

· No silent mutation: every change is journaled; by NSM-1 and NSM-2, no distinction can disappear without a recorded Act.
· No semantic collapse: by the type system of §14, distinction erasure is forbidden; the type checker rejects any Act that would collapse distinctions.
· No false merge: by the reconciliation of §10, all admitted distinctions are preserved.

Thus \Delta(EB_0) \subseteq \Delta(EB_n) (no distinction is erased), and since the transformation sequence is monotone (no new distinctions are created by the Acts), \Delta(EB_0) = \Delta(EB_n). Hence |\Delta(EB_0) \cap \Delta(EB_n)| = |\Delta(EB_0)|, so \Phi = 1. ∎

Corollary. Any violation of the three conditions produces \Phi(EB_0, EB_n) < 1, with deficit bounded by the number of erased distinctions.

