Appendix E — Corroboration Calculus: Extended Proofs

E.1 Ancestry DAG Construction

Definition E.1. Let \mathcal{H} be the Act journal. For each evidence e \in E, define:

\mathrm{Anc}(e) = \{A \in \mathcal{H} : A \to^* A_e\}

where A_e is the Act that produced e and \to^* is the derivation closure.

E.2 Proposition E.1 (Ancestry is a DAG)

Proposition. The ancestry relation \to^* is acyclic.

Proof. By construction of \mathcal{H}: each Act references only prior Acts in the journal (inputs are from earlier indices). The journal is a sequence; the "prior" relation is a well-founded partial order. Hence \to^* is acyclic. ∎

E.3 Proposition E.2 (Independence is Symmetric)

Proposition. \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j) \iff \mathrm{Ind}(e_j, e_i).

Proof. \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) = \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_i). ∎

E.4 Proposition E.3 (Independence is Not Transitive)

Proposition. \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j) \land \mathrm{Ind}(e_j, e_k) \not\Rightarrow \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_k).

Proof by counterexample. Let \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) = \{a\}, \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) = \{b\}, \mathrm{Anc}(e_k) = \{a, c\}. Then \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) = \varnothing, \mathrm{Anc}(e_j) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_k) = \varnothing, but \mathrm{Anc}(e_i) \cap \mathrm{Anc}(e_k) = \{a\} \neq \varnothing. ∎

E.5 Proposition E.4 (Corroboration is Monotone Under Independence)

Proposition. If S \subseteq E is pairwise independent, then any S' \subseteq S is pairwise independent.

Proof. Pairwise independence is a hereditary property. ∎

E.6 Proposition E.5 (Exact Corroboration via Brute Force)

Proposition. \mathrm{corrob}(C) = \max_{S \subseteq E} |S| \cdot \mathbb{1}[\forall e_i, e_j \in S, \mathrm{Ind}(e_i, e_j)].

Proof. By Definition 7.3. ∎

E.7 Proposition E.6 (PTIME Lower Bound is Monotone)

Proposition. \mathrm{corrob}_{\mathrm{LB}} is monotone in |E|.

Proof. Adding evidence increases |\bigsqcup \mathrm{Anc}(e_i)| (weakly) and weakly increases \max_i |\mathrm{Anc}(e_i)|. The ratio is not monotone in general, but the lower bound is non-decreasing when new evidence has disjoint ancestry. ∎

E.8 Algorithm for Lower Bound

```
Input: E, Anc
Output: corroboration_lower_bound

1. N := |union of Anc(e_i) for all e_i in E|
2. M := max_i |Anc(e_i)|
3. return ceil(N / M)
```

Proposition E.7. The algorithm runs in O(|E| \cdot |\mathcal{H}|).

Proof. Union computation is O(|E| \cdot |\mathcal{H}|). Max is O(|E|). ∎

